        {
          "insert": "Board Prioritization"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-board-prioritization-ca-2": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-board-prioritization-ca-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Board prioritization, candidate-prior scoring, and deterministic scheduler signals."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-board-helpers-produce-de-3": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-board-helpers-produce-de-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Board helpers produce deterministic candidate-prior features. The run scheduler\nuses those features as rank input, while final admission follows explicit epoch,\nworker-pool, claim, cooldown, and exhaustion policy."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-the-helper-score-is-grap-4": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-the-helper-score-is-grap-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The helper score is graph-first. It should surface places where a worker is\nlikely to create reusable information, propagate a source-shape fact, or unlock a\ncluster of related targets. Local closeness to 100% is still useful, but it is a\nbounded high-accuracy bonus rather than proof that the missing information is\navailable."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-snapshot-and-admission-s-5": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-snapshot-and-admission-s-5",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Snapshot And Admission Semantics"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-loadboardsnapshot-read-6": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-loadboardsnapshot-read-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "loadBoardSnapshot",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " reads the current "
        },
        {
          "insert": "build/GALE01/report.json",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "objdiff.json",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", builds imperfect-function candidates, scores them with the\nhelper prior, and returns the highest-ranked candidate window. The snapshot is a\nfresh read of the available board artifacts. The trigger does not run the Melee\nbuild or objdiff pipeline itself; when those artifacts are regenerated, the next\nsnapshot observes the updated scores."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-when-a-resource-graph-da-7": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-when-a-resource-graph-da-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "When a resource graph database is available, the snapshot ranks against that\ngraph before sorting. Graph ranking can remove candidates whose file is\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "read_only_complete",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " or "
        },
        {
          "insert": "blocked",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". Remaining candidates carry a\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "rank",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " breakdown with closeness, information gain, completion readiness, unlock\npotential, context quality, risk, graph score, high-accuracy bonuses, and final\npriority. When the graph is not available, candidates still rank deterministically\nfrom compressed local closeness."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-the-runtime-uses-two-dif-8": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-the-runtime-uses-two-dif-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The runtime uses two different limits:"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-candidate-window-how-ma-9": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-candidate-window-how-ma-9",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Candidate window: how many ranked board candidates are inspected during"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-scheduler-admission-10": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-scheduler-admission-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "  scheduler admission."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-epoch-size-how-many-tar-11": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-epoch-size-how-many-tar-11",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Epoch size: how many targets are admitted to the active scheduler epoch."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-worker-pool-size-how-ma-12": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-worker-pool-size-how-ma-12",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Worker pool size: how many target claims the run loop tries to keep active."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-for-example-a-run-can-a-13": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-for-example-a-run-can-a-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "For example, a run can admit a 256-target epoch while keeping 64 worker slots\nactive and scanning a 512-target candidate window. As workers claim admitted\nwork, the active pool draws from the fixed admitted set. Fast run-evidence\nrefresh can update priorities for admitted-but-unclaimed targets, so new\nknowledge can change claim order without injecting accidental out-of-epoch work."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-candidate-prior-14": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-candidate-prior-14",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Candidate Prior"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-text-15": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-text-15",
      "type": "code",
      "props": {
        "language": "text"
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "candidate_prior =\n  information_priority_score\n  + high_accuracy_bonus\n  + accuracy_readiness_bonus\n  + closeness_fallback_score"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-information-priority-sc-16": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-information-priority-sc-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "information_priority_score",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is the graph-first admission component. It weights\ninformation gain, unlock potential, completion readiness, and context quality\nahead of local fuzzy closeness, then subtracts risk. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "completion_readiness_score",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "\nasks whether there is actionable evidence available: tool findings, path facts,\nhistorical lessons, curated lessons, duplicate references, matched siblings, and\nrelevant PRs. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "closeness_score",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is a capped, log-compressed version of the old\nsize/fuzzy helper score. It produces a small "
        },
        {
          "insert": "high_accuracy_bonus",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", plus an\nextra "
        },
        {
          "insert": "accuracy_readiness_bonus",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " when a near-finished target also has strong\nreadiness or information signals. When no graph information signal is present,\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "closeness_fallback_score",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " keeps the target in a low-priority lane but spreads\nthat lane by raw closeness, fuzzy gap, and size so the fallback does not\ncollapse into a flat tie."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-this-means-a-context-poo-17": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-this-means-a-context-poo-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "This means a context-poor 99.x% target should not outrank a lower-fuzzy target\nthat is likely to add reusable knowledge. The best first targets are high\ninformation and high readiness; high closeness is a multiplier when the graph\nalso says there is useful evidence to exploit. When graph information signals\nare absent, closeness-only targets are kept as a low fallback rather than a\nprimary admission lane, with enough internal spread to make their order\ninspectable."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-signals-18": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-signals-18",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Signals"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-signal-why-it-matter-19": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-signal-why-it-matter-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "| Signal | Why It Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Matched duplicate ref | A matched source shape can be adapted across unrelated files when assembly shape supports it. |\n| Graph degree | A target connected to many similar functions can propagate more facts if solved or partially improved. |\n| Linked incomplete functions | Sibling or connected imperfect functions can benefit from a fact discovered while investigating this target. |\n| Worker context quality | Nearby matched siblings, graph edges, or reducer facts make deep worker research more grounded. |\n| Recent stalls | Repeated no-delta attempts should cool down the target unless new facts arrive. |\n| Data/rodata risk | Header, static, section-order, split, and relocation-sensitive work needs slower validation and fewer parallel edits. |"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-scheduler-contract-20": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-scheduler-contract-20",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Scheduler Contract"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-the-scheduler-receives-t-21": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-the-scheduler-receives-t-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The scheduler receives the ranked board plus durable run state and admits a\nfixed epoch set. A high prior is not an instruction to edit. It is a claim that\nthis target may produce leverage; deterministic policy can admit it, defer it\nbehind cooldown or later-epoch policy, or let boundary routing move it into a repair,\nfact/research, or stalled lane."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-parallel-capacity-signal-22": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-parallel-capacity-signal-22",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Parallel Capacity Signal"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-15-board-prioritization-raw-admitted-target-dept-23": {
      "id": "b-15-board-prioritization-raw-admitted-target-dept-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Raw admitted-target depth is not enough for a parallel run. The scheduler also\ntracks active claims and open worker slots so the pool does not look healthy\nonly because many targets exist on paper. Epoch admission prefers fresh\ngraph-ranked candidates, while low admitted-target or active-claim pressure can\nemit "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pool_below_target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " for deterministic admission/backoff handling."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
